Use a scheduler loop that sleeps until the next absolute deadline, not “sleep 1” each time.

Example in Perl:

```perl
#!/usr/bin/perl
use strict;
use warnings;
use Time::HiRes qw(time sleep);

my $interval = shift @ARGV;
my $start = time();

while (1) {
    system(@ARGV);
    $start += $interval;
    sleep($start - time());
}
```

Run it like:

```sh
perl timer.pl 1 date '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S'
```

This avoids accumulating drift from command runtime. If you need true precision, use a small C program or cron/systemd timers, but for shell use this is the usual solution.

