If your CSV is comma-separated and you want fields based on `from=` / `to=`, awk can do this with `-F,` and `split()`:

```sh
awk -F, '{
  split($2,a,"=");
  print a[2], $3, $2
  split($6,b,"=");
  print b[2], $7, $6
}' data.txt
```

If you want one unique row per `from` or `to`, use an associative array:

```sh
awk -F, '{
  split($2,a,"="); arr[a[2]]=a[2]" = "$3","$2
  split($6,b,"="); arr[b[2]]=b[2]" = "$7","$6
}
END { for (k in arr) print arr[k] }' data.txt
```

For only `to=` values, use the `$6/$7` part only.

If you want to batch-rename files while preserving the extension, including filenames with spaces, use a loop that safely quotes filenames.

Perl example:

```bash
perl -e '
  @files = sort glob("*");
  $counter = 0;
  for (@files) {
    next unless -f $_;
    ($ext = $_) =~ s/.*\.([^.]*)$/$1/;
    $new = sprintf("%04d", ++$counter) . ($ext ? ".$ext" : "");
    die "Target exists: $new\n" if -e $new;
    rename $_, $new or die "rename failed: $!";
  }
'
```

This sorts by filename, handles spaces, and keeps extensions. Test on copies first. If you want, I can also give you a safer Bash version.

You can do this in Perl by keeping the needed value from the current record and applying it to the next one.

A simple pattern based on your example:

```perl
#!/usr/bin/perl
use strict;
use warnings;

my $prev_val = '';

while (my $line = <>) {
    chomp $line;

    # Extract value from current record (adjust pattern/position as needed)
    if ($line =~ /^MTD.{8}(...)(.*?) /) {
        $prev_val = $1;   # or combine with $2 if needed
    }

    # Append previous value to subsequent records
    if ($line =~ /^AA |^BB /) {
        $line .= $prev_val if length $prev_val;
    }

    print "$line\n";
}
```

If you want to write to another file:

```perl
open my $in,  '<', 'input.txt'  or die $!;
open my $out, '>', 'output.txt' or die $!;

my $prev_val = '';

while (my $line = <$in>) {
    chomp $line;

    if ($line =~ /^MTD.{8}(...)(.*?) /) {
        $prev_val = $1;
    }

    $line .= $prev_val if $line =~ /^AA |^BB /;
    print $out "$line\n";
}
```

The canonical Bash way is to use `getopts` in a `while` loop, then validate each option yourself inside the `case` arms. `getopts` already handles order-independent short options, so `-a foo -b bar` and `-b bar -a foo` both work.

Typical pattern:

```bash
while getopts ":a:b:c:" opt; do
  case "$opt" in
    a) [[ -n $OPTARG ]] || { echo "missing -a value"; exit 2; }
       A=$OPTARG ;;
    b) B=$OPTARG ;;
    c) C=$OPTARG ;;
    :) echo "Option -$OPTARG requires an argument"; exit 2 ;;
    \?) echo "Unknown option: -$OPTARG"; exit 2 ;;
  esac
done
shift $((OPTIND-1))
```

If you need to reject values starting with `-`, check `OPTARG` before assigning.

The canonical way in bash is to use `getopts` for short options, because it handles options in any order and separates them from positional arguments.

Typical pattern:

```bash
while getopts ":ab:c" opt; do
  case "$opt" in
    a) flag_a=true ;;
    b) value_b="$OPTARG" ;;
    c) flag_c=true ;;
    \?) echo "Invalid option: -$OPTARG" ;;
    :)  echo "Option -$OPTARG requires an argument" ;;
  esac
done
shift $((OPTIND - 1))
```

If you need long options or more complex parsing, common tools are `getopt` or manual argument parsing with a loop.

