For a Linux input device, the “driver” is identified by the kernel/input subsystem fields you set on the `input_dev`, not just by `EV_REL` capabilities.

Set at least:

- `name`
- `phys`
- `id` fields (`bustype`, `vendor`, `product`, `version`)
- proper capability bits (`evbit`, `keybit`, `relbit`)

Example:

```c
vms_input_dev->name = "Virtual Mouse";
vms_input_dev->phys = "vmd/input0";
vms_input_dev->id.bustype = BUS_VIRTUAL;
vms_input_dev->id.vendor  = 0x0000;
vms_input_dev->id.product = 0x0000;
vms_input_dev->id.version = 0x0000;

vms_input_dev->evbit[0] = BIT_MASK(EV_KEY) | BIT_MASK(EV_REL);
vms_input_dev->keybit[BIT_WORD(BTN_MOUSE)] =
    BIT_MASK(BTN_LEFT) | BIT_MASK(BTN_RIGHT) | BIT_MASK(BTN_MIDDLE);
vms_input_dev->relbit[0] = BIT_MASK(REL_X) | BIT_MASK(REL_Y);
```

This helps Xorg/udev recognize it instead of ignoring it.

