A simple, fairly reliable check is to inspect PID 1:

```bash
init=$(ps -p 1 -o comm= 2>/dev/null)
case "$init" in
  systemd) echo systemd ;;
  init)    echo sysvinit/other ;;
  upstart) echo upstart ;;
  *)       echo "unknown: $init" ;;
esac
```

But don’t rely on only one test. `/proc/1/comm` or `ps` can be misleading if `init` is symlinked/renamed. Also, having `systemd` installed doesn’t mean it’s the active init.

For portable shell, bash is not guaranteed on all Unix-like systems. Prefer POSIX `sh` if you need broad portability. If you only care about installing init scripts, detecting the script format you need may be better than detecting the init system directly.

