If your input is in blocks like the example, Perl paragraph mode is a good fit.

Save this as `transpose.pl`:

```perl
#!/usr/bin/env perl
use strict;
use warnings;

my %final_lines;
my $filename = shift // die "No input file given!\n";
open my $IN, '<', $filename or die "Failed to open $filename: $!\n";

local $/ = "";   # paragraph mode
while (<$IN>) {
    my @lines  = split /\n/;
    my @fields = split /\s+/, shift @lines;
    my $line_number = 0;

    for my $line (@lines) {
        my @data = split /\s+/, $line;
        for my $i (0..$#data) {
            $final_lines{$fields[$i]}->[$line_number] += $data[$i];
        }
        $line_number++;
    }
}

for my $key (sort { $a <=> $b } keys %final_lines) {
    print $key, ' ', @{ $final_lines{$key} }, "\n";
}
```

Run it with:

```sh
perl transpose.pl input_file > output_file
```

