In awk, use a captured group replacement via gawk’s `gensub()`, not plain `gsub()`.

Example:

```sh
awk '{
  n = gensub(/.*#([0-9]+)".*/, "\\1", "g")
  if (n ~ /^[0-9]+$/)
    gsub(/[0-9]+"/, n+11 "\"")
  print
}' file
```

Better, if you need to change both numbers on each line, use field splitting:

```sh
awk 'BEGIN{FS=OFS="\" \"#"}
{
  if (NF < 2) { print; next }
  a = gensub(/.* ([0-9]+)$/, "\\1", "g", $1)
  b = gensub(/([0-9]+)\"/, "\\1", "g", $2)
  gsub(/[0-9]+$/, a+11, $1)
  gsub(/[0-9]+$/, b+11, $2)
  print $1 FS $2
}' file
```

Note: `\1` in awk strings must be written as `"\\1"`.

